/**
 * The drivable road network packaged for map rendering, plus the provenance a viewer
 * needs in order to read the map correctly.
 *
 * The network is stored as directed edges — two {@link RoadSegment} rows for a two-way
 * street — which is right for routing and wrong for drawing, since it would paint every
 * two-way street twice. This type collapses reverse pairs back to one polyline per
 * physical stretch of road, roughly halving what crosses the wire.
 *
 * The polylines travel as a single JSON string in {@link MapNetwork#linesJson} rather
 * than as thousands of typed objects: the payload is dominated by coordinates, and a
 * positional array avoids repeating field names tens of thousands of times.
 */
type MapNetwork {

  /**
   * One entry per physical road stretch, as a JSON array of positional arrays:
   * `[roadClass, streetName, isBridge, isOneway, speedIsEstimated, speedKph,
   * [[lat, lon], ...], congestionPercent, isClosed]`, where the flags are 1 or 0.
   * Positional rather than keyed purely to keep the payload small.
   *
   * The last two entries are what the requested {@link Scenario} does to this stretch
   * of road:
   *
   * - `congestionPercent` — how fast traffic moves here as a percentage of free flow,
   *   so 100 is unaffected and 28 means traffic is crawling at just over a quarter of
   *   normal speed. It is the product of every zone and road multiplier covering this
   *   stretch, and deliberately EXCLUDES {@link Scenario#ambulanceSpeedFactor}: this
   *   number describes the traffic a viewer can see, not the privileged speed an
   *   ambulance is assumed to manage through it.
   * - `isClosed` — 1 when the scenario removes this stretch from the graph entirely.
   *   Closed stretches are still drawn, because a closure the viewer cannot see is
   *   indistinguishable from a road that was never there.
   *
   * Under the baseline scenario every entry is `100, 0`.
   */
  linesJson: !string

  /**
   * Number of polylines in {@link MapNetwork#linesJson} after collapsing reverse pairs.
   */
  lineCount: int

  /**
   * Number of directed {@link RoadSegment} edges these polylines were built from —
   * larger than {@link MapNetwork#lineCount} by the number of two-way streets.
   */
  segmentCount: int

  /**
   * Number of {@link RoadNode} intersections in the routable graph.
   */
  nodeCount: int

  /**
   * Directed edges tagged as bridges. Note this over-counts water crossings, since
   * OpenStreetMap also tags railway overpasses and grade separations `bridge=yes`.
   */
  bridgeCount: int

  /**
   * Directed edges whose speed was estimated from road class because no `maxspeed` tag
   * exists upstream. In this extract these outnumber the observed ones roughly two to
   * one, which is the single most important caveat on any travel time shown.
   */
  estimatedSpeedCount: int

  /**
   * Directed edges whose speed came from an OpenStreetMap `maxspeed` tag.
   */
  observedSpeedCount: int

  /**
   * Attribution text that must be displayed wherever this data is drawn, as required by
   * the Open Database Licence.
   */
  attribution: !string

  /**
   * Southern edge of the modelled core area, for fitting the initial map view.
   */
  bboxSouth: double

  /**
   * Northern edge of the modelled core area.
   */
  bboxNorth: double

  /**
   * Western edge of the modelled core area.
   */
  bboxWest: double

  /**
   * Eastern edge of the modelled core area.
   */
  bboxEast: double

  /**
   * Id of the {@link Scenario} the congestion figures in
   * {@link MapNetwork#linesJson} were computed under. Always populated — a caller that
   * supplied no id gets the baseline's id back rather than null, so a client can never
   * display traffic colours without being able to say which conditions produced them.
   */
  scenarioId: string

  /**
   * Human-readable name of that scenario, so the client can label the view without a
   * second fetch.
   */
  scenarioName: string

  /**
   * Polylines this scenario closes outright. Zero under the baseline.
   */
  closedLineCount: int

  /**
   * Polylines this scenario slows without closing — anything whose congestion is below
   * free flow. Zero under the baseline.
   */
  slowedLineCount: int
}
